WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.237
(whooshing)

00:00:03.237 --> 00:00:04.349
- Good morning, Believe Nation,

00:00:04.349 --> 00:00:05.525
my name is Evan Carmichael,

00:00:05.525 --> 00:00:07.249
my one word is believe,

00:00:07.249 --> 00:00:08.765
and I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:08.765 --> 00:00:11.650
are going to solve all the
world's major problems.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:13.287
I started the Believe Life series

00:00:13.287 --> 00:00:16.574
to try to expand beyond just
the world of entrepreneurship

00:00:16.574 --> 00:00:19.960
and try to learn some other
skills, resources, ideas,

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.334
to help us become a more
complete human being.

00:00:23.334 --> 00:00:27.255
Today we're going to learn
some hiking tips for beginners.

00:00:27.255 --> 00:00:28.093
And as always guys,

00:00:28.093 --> 00:00:29.863
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:31.887
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:31.887 --> 00:00:34.486
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired.

00:00:34.486 --> 00:00:35.826
And when you write it down

00:00:35.826 --> 00:00:39.019
it's much more likely to
stick with yourself as well.

00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:39.852
Enjoy.

00:00:39.852 --> 00:00:43.269
(bouncy uplifting music)

00:00:52.694 --> 00:00:56.027
(light uplifting music)

00:00:57.701 --> 00:00:59.142
- Do you ever wonder what separates

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:01.089
those living an elite life

00:01:01.089 --> 00:01:03.783
from those who live a mediocre one?

00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:05.171
Do you think it's their bank account?

00:01:05.171 --> 00:01:06.699
Guess again.

00:01:06.699 --> 00:01:09.044
They make time for adventure.

00:01:09.044 --> 00:01:09.877
Make.

00:01:09.877 --> 00:01:11.018
Time.

00:01:11.018 --> 00:01:12.123
For adventure.

00:01:12.123 --> 00:01:14.253
Don't get me wrong, there's many things

00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:17.423
that separate an elite
life from a mediocre one,

00:01:17.423 --> 00:01:21.883
but today I want to talk about
memories and experiences.

00:01:21.883 --> 00:01:24.215
These are things that we all have, right?

00:01:24.215 --> 00:01:26.903
But at what level and what frequency?

00:01:26.903 --> 00:01:29.644
Ultimately guys, life, in my opinion

00:01:29.644 --> 00:01:32.553
is and should be an adventure.

00:01:32.553 --> 00:01:34.071
Over the past year and a half

00:01:34.071 --> 00:01:37.241
I've studied countless
world-class leaders,

00:01:37.241 --> 00:01:39.821
and something that I've noticed
they all have in common,

00:01:39.821 --> 00:01:43.006
that they constantly create
memorable experiences.

00:01:43.006 --> 00:01:47.173
In 2015, I made it a point
to make time for adventure.

00:01:50.107 --> 00:01:52.062
And during the summer of 2015,

00:01:52.062 --> 00:01:55.017
I probably fit in more adventures
than I've probably put in

00:01:55.017 --> 00:01:56.315
in my entire life.

00:01:56.315 --> 00:01:57.719
And it was life-changing.

00:01:57.719 --> 00:02:01.122
Don't get me wrong, I've
always loved hiking,

00:02:01.122 --> 00:02:03.111
but I never found the time to do it

00:02:03.111 --> 00:02:05.288
until I changed my mindset.

00:02:05.288 --> 00:02:08.122
I ultimately decided to make time,

00:02:08.122 --> 00:02:10.938
and I planned a new place
to explore every week.

00:02:10.938 --> 00:02:14.515
And I planned my life and work around it.

00:02:14.515 --> 00:02:17.168
Some trips were day hikes,
some were half-days.

00:02:17.168 --> 00:02:18.754
Some were weekends.

00:02:18.754 --> 00:02:20.738
Some were four days.

00:02:20.738 --> 00:02:22.938
The point being, no matter
what my schedule was,

00:02:22.938 --> 00:02:24.457
I always found time,

00:02:24.457 --> 00:02:26.842
whether it be a long or short adventure,

00:02:26.842 --> 00:02:30.215
and I've honestly never
been more fulfilled

00:02:30.215 --> 00:02:32.183
or happier in my life.

00:02:32.183 --> 00:02:33.998
I mean really, what's the
point of doing anything

00:02:33.998 --> 00:02:35.566
if you're not having fun

00:02:35.566 --> 00:02:38.393
and it doesn't elevate you emotionally?

00:02:38.393 --> 00:02:42.473
Now, I know we all have things
that we must do, day to day,

00:02:42.473 --> 00:02:45.898
that may not be fun, can get a bit bland,

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:49.149
so why not make time to
take a mini adventure,

00:02:49.149 --> 00:02:52.972
a half day or a weekend,
every week, every month.

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:54.972
This will snap you out of any complacency

00:02:54.972 --> 00:02:56.130
you may be feeling.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.374
Don't let yourself be complacent.

00:02:58.374 --> 00:03:02.169
Complacency, it's the killer
of dreams and ambition.

00:03:02.169 --> 00:03:04.367
You can start right now

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:06.798
by making a list of adventures
that you want to take

00:03:06.798 --> 00:03:09.298
throughout the rest of the year.

00:03:09.298 --> 00:03:12.735
(light uplifting music)

00:03:12.735 --> 00:03:13.760
- Hi, my name's John

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.107
and today we're going to do
a little beginner's intro

00:03:17.107 --> 00:03:18.324
to hiking.

00:03:18.324 --> 00:03:20.468
And one of the things
that I love about hiking

00:03:20.468 --> 00:03:23.414
is that it truly is very, very simple.

00:03:23.414 --> 00:03:25.138
You put one foot in front of the other,

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:27.537
you get out into a beautiful
natural area like this,

00:03:27.537 --> 00:03:29.784
and that is the essence of hiking.

00:03:29.784 --> 00:03:31.921
Of course, there are a
few tips and suggestions

00:03:31.921 --> 00:03:34.204
that will make the beginner's experience

00:03:34.204 --> 00:03:35.659
a little bit easier,

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:37.854
and will avoid some of the common pitfalls

00:03:37.854 --> 00:03:39.996
that people without experience come across

00:03:39.996 --> 00:03:41.177
when they go outside.

00:03:41.177 --> 00:03:44.190
First things first, I always
like to talk about the feet.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.432
Make sure that you have
appropriate footwear on.

00:03:46.432 --> 00:03:48.376
These boots that I'm wearing today

00:03:48.376 --> 00:03:50.955
are more of my heavier-duty hiking boots.

00:03:50.955 --> 00:03:54.134
It is also appropriate if
you're going on easier terrain

00:03:54.134 --> 00:03:56.924
to use running shoes or
lightweight hiking shoes,

00:03:56.924 --> 00:04:00.933
even Tevas or other hiking-style
sandals can be adequate

00:04:00.933 --> 00:04:02.118
if you know what you're doing

00:04:02.118 --> 00:04:04.132
and the terrain is more forgiving.

00:04:04.132 --> 00:04:07.819
For dress, I always like to
dress particularly lightly.

00:04:07.819 --> 00:04:11.259
Shorts, lightweight long pants, a t-shirt,

00:04:11.259 --> 00:04:13.277
and maybe a jacket if it's a cooler day

00:04:13.277 --> 00:04:14.642
that you might store in your pack

00:04:14.642 --> 00:04:15.572
if you start out and it's warmer

00:04:15.572 --> 00:04:17.258
but it might be raining later on.

00:04:17.258 --> 00:04:19.931
As far as carrying some things with you,

00:04:19.931 --> 00:04:22.118
some very essential or basic things.

00:04:22.118 --> 00:04:25.264
A day pack is really good for
your shorter hiking trips,

00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:28.193
and in that day pack, or
carrying with you, have water.

00:04:28.193 --> 00:04:30.425
Please bring plenty of water.

00:04:30.425 --> 00:04:32.128
If you're going to go for an hour or two,

00:04:32.128 --> 00:04:33.679
one water bottle's sufficient.

00:04:33.679 --> 00:04:37.328
Multiple hour hikes will
take multiple water bottles.

00:04:37.328 --> 00:04:39.045
A little bit of food, some snacks.

00:04:39.045 --> 00:04:42.035
Good old raisins and peanuts, aka gorp,

00:04:42.035 --> 00:04:43.519
and then just an extra jacket.

00:04:43.519 --> 00:04:44.622
Always be prepared

00:04:44.622 --> 00:04:46.324
even if there's no rain in the forecast

00:04:46.324 --> 00:04:47.649
it might rain on you,

00:04:47.649 --> 00:04:50.576
I always bring an extra jacket like that.

00:04:50.576 --> 00:04:52.576
First aid kit, sunblock,

00:04:54.775 --> 00:04:56.616
those are pretty much the essentials.

00:04:56.616 --> 00:04:58.656
As far as planning a place to go hiking,

00:04:58.656 --> 00:05:01.683
almost anybody in this country
lives near a beautiful park

00:05:01.683 --> 00:05:03.395
or a wild natural area.

00:05:03.395 --> 00:05:06.758
Today we're in Montana de Oro
State Park here in California.

00:05:06.758 --> 00:05:08.777
As you can see, thousands of acres,

00:05:08.777 --> 00:05:11.024
wonderful hiking trails here.

00:05:11.024 --> 00:05:13.018
If you're beginning, I
would pick out a trail

00:05:13.018 --> 00:05:14.662
that might be two or
three miles in length,

00:05:14.662 --> 00:05:16.888
maybe a little bit shorter,
pretty moderate terrain.

00:05:16.888 --> 00:05:18.641
As you get used to it, you can work up

00:05:18.641 --> 00:05:22.808
and do heavier-duty trails
with more up-and-down terrain.

00:05:24.271 --> 00:05:26.584
Eight or 10 miles with
a lot of up-and-down

00:05:26.584 --> 00:05:29.051
would be a really full
hiking day for folks.

00:05:29.051 --> 00:05:30.714
That's hiking in a nutshell,

00:05:30.714 --> 00:05:33.135
and I hope that that helps
beginners get a feel for it

00:05:33.135 --> 00:05:34.831
and avoid some of the common problems

00:05:34.831 --> 00:05:35.708
that happen to folks

00:05:35.708 --> 00:05:36.872
who don't really know what they're doing

00:05:36.872 --> 00:05:37.935
out there while they're hiking.

00:05:37.935 --> 00:05:38.979
- Have a plan.

00:05:38.979 --> 00:05:42.404
99% of the time that I
travel in the backcountry,

00:05:42.404 --> 00:05:44.017
I do so alone.

00:05:44.017 --> 00:05:45.986
Somethin' I just really enjoy.

00:05:45.986 --> 00:05:48.912
So it's really important,
on the front end of my trips

00:05:48.912 --> 00:05:50.535
that I have a plan.

00:05:50.535 --> 00:05:53.595
And so what I do is I
do some research online,

00:05:53.595 --> 00:05:56.219
look at websites at the
area I'm going to be in,

00:05:56.219 --> 00:05:59.124
I look at a map to see where
I'm going to park my truck.

00:05:59.124 --> 00:06:01.728
I look at the various
destinations I want to get to,

00:06:01.728 --> 00:06:03.619
how long, how many
miles it's going to take

00:06:03.619 --> 00:06:04.452
for me to get there,

00:06:04.452 --> 00:06:06.718
how long it's going to
take to hike those miles.

00:06:06.718 --> 00:06:09.028
And the other thing I look at is weather

00:06:09.028 --> 00:06:10.941
so that I can anticipate

00:06:10.941 --> 00:06:13.411
if there's going to be any
precipitation, rain, or snow,

00:06:13.411 --> 00:06:16.656
and I know the predicted high temperatures

00:06:16.656 --> 00:06:19.578
and the low temperatures
so I can pack accordingly.

00:06:19.578 --> 00:06:22.624
I always carry a compass and a map

00:06:22.624 --> 00:06:24.580
when I'm traveling in the backcountry.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:28.331
What I do is I take
the National Geographic

00:06:28.331 --> 00:06:30.474
Trails Illustrated map,

00:06:30.474 --> 00:06:31.890
it's a big color map,

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.380
and I'll take photocopies of the sections

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:36.530
or the areas I'm going to be hiking in,

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:39.872
and I put those in a gallon Ziploc bag.

00:06:39.872 --> 00:06:41.340
It allows me to travel lighter,

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.811
it protects those documents,

00:06:42.811 --> 00:06:44.400
I can write on the maps

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.425
and not damage the original map,

00:06:46.425 --> 00:06:47.731
and it's very lightweight

00:06:47.731 --> 00:06:49.710
for me to travel in the
backcountry that way.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.047
And also I carry my iPhone

00:06:52.047 --> 00:06:55.964
which has a GPS built
into it, with a Topo app.

00:06:56.879 --> 00:07:00.798
And I just run the phone in airplane mode

00:07:00.798 --> 00:07:02.471
and so it conserves battery power

00:07:02.471 --> 00:07:04.482
and I pull that out every once in a while

00:07:04.482 --> 00:07:07.316
if I want to double check where I am at.

00:07:07.316 --> 00:07:10.137
But primarily, I use my map and compass.

00:07:10.137 --> 00:07:14.267
I leave a detailed itinerary
with at least two people.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.264
One is always my wife,

00:07:16.264 --> 00:07:18.333
I tell here exactly where
I'm going to park my pickup

00:07:18.333 --> 00:07:20.977
and what my plan is for
while I'm in the backcountry,

00:07:20.977 --> 00:07:22.744
where I'm going to be and so on,

00:07:22.744 --> 00:07:24.860
and the other person I
leave this information with

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:26.424
is a good friend of mine

00:07:26.424 --> 00:07:29.443
who knows the area and has
the ability to come and get me

00:07:29.443 --> 00:07:31.540
if I run into trouble.

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:33.516
Most of the time when
you read about someone

00:07:33.516 --> 00:07:36.007
who's experienced a
tragedy in the backcountry

00:07:36.007 --> 00:07:37.058
while traveling solo,

00:07:37.058 --> 00:07:40.278
it's because they didn't
tell anybody where they went.

00:07:40.278 --> 00:07:41.620
- Day hikes.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:46.023
This portion of the video is
going to be for day hikes that are

00:07:46.023 --> 00:07:47.805
one, two, three miles.

00:07:47.805 --> 00:07:48.644
If I'm goin' on a day hike

00:07:48.644 --> 00:07:50.275
that's one, two, or three miles

00:07:50.275 --> 00:07:52.290
these are the items
that I'm going to bring.

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:55.119
First thing I focus on, food and water.

00:07:55.119 --> 00:07:56.926
What I have here today,

00:07:56.926 --> 00:07:58.837
it's perfect for one, two, three miles,

00:07:58.837 --> 00:08:00.457
sometimes I'll bring a little more.

00:08:00.457 --> 00:08:03.260
I just have some granola and some peanuts,

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:04.474
simple snack to snack on.

00:08:04.474 --> 00:08:06.598
Sometimes I'll bring extra granola bars,

00:08:06.598 --> 00:08:08.601
sometimes even a can of soup,

00:08:08.601 --> 00:08:11.684
depending on the area that I'm going.

00:08:12.552 --> 00:08:13.440
So that's food.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.895
Water, usually I'll
just bring one canteen,

00:08:15.895 --> 00:08:19.086
and if I'm going for longer distances,

00:08:19.086 --> 00:08:20.929
usually I'll bring a bladder pack

00:08:20.929 --> 00:08:23.844
or if I know the area that I'm goin' in,

00:08:23.844 --> 00:08:26.517
I'll bring a water filter.

00:08:26.517 --> 00:08:28.663
This way I can fill up my canteen

00:08:28.663 --> 00:08:32.862
when I stop near a stream
or somethin' like that.

00:08:32.862 --> 00:08:34.411
So that's food and water covered

00:08:34.411 --> 00:08:37.031
for a one to three mile hike.

00:08:37.031 --> 00:08:38.883
Protection from the elements,

00:08:38.883 --> 00:08:41.633
usually I'll just bring a poncho,

00:08:42.733 --> 00:08:45.724
and this will basically keep me dry

00:08:45.724 --> 00:08:48.012
and all my gear that I have dry.

00:08:48.012 --> 00:08:51.193
A cheap alternative
would be a big trash bag.

00:08:51.193 --> 00:08:52.728
You can get them fairly cheap,

00:08:52.728 --> 00:08:54.521
just cut a hole in the
top and put your arms in

00:08:54.521 --> 00:08:56.393
and there you go, you got your own poncho,

00:08:56.393 --> 00:08:58.481
very lightweight and cheap.

00:08:58.481 --> 00:09:01.227
I would recommend not
buying dollar store ponchos

00:09:01.227 --> 00:09:03.048
'cause they tear very easily.

00:09:03.048 --> 00:09:06.141
If you're goin' in an area
where there's a small footpath

00:09:06.141 --> 00:09:08.214
and you scrape up against a branch,

00:09:08.214 --> 00:09:10.129
chances are the poncho's going to tear

00:09:10.129 --> 00:09:11.557
if you buy the cheap-quality ones.

00:09:11.557 --> 00:09:13.341
So I'd recommend maybe gettin' one

00:09:13.341 --> 00:09:14.575
that's a little more high-quality

00:09:14.575 --> 00:09:17.151
than the dollar store brand.

00:09:17.151 --> 00:09:18.677
so that's protection from the elements

00:09:18.677 --> 00:09:21.020
for a one to three mile hike.

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:24.313
What I like to call just-in-case items.

00:09:24.313 --> 00:09:26.625
Again, fire starting,

00:09:26.625 --> 00:09:29.465
if for some reason you do get lost.

00:09:29.465 --> 00:09:31.610
Very simple, bring a
lighter, bring two lighters,

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:32.973
bring three lighters,

00:09:32.973 --> 00:09:34.428
keep 'em in different locations,

00:09:34.428 --> 00:09:37.616
because if for some reason
you lose your backpack

00:09:37.616 --> 00:09:39.337
and all your fire stuff
is in the backpack,

00:09:39.337 --> 00:09:40.399
what are you going to do?

00:09:40.399 --> 00:09:42.389
So keep a lighter in your pocket,

00:09:42.389 --> 00:09:43.684
maybe keep a lighter in your jacket,

00:09:43.684 --> 00:09:46.179
I have another one in
my inside pocket here,

00:09:46.179 --> 00:09:49.665
and keep a couple in your backpack.

00:09:49.665 --> 00:09:52.459
Practice usin' a ferro rod of course.

00:09:52.459 --> 00:09:53.546
But that's the basics of fire,

00:09:53.546 --> 00:09:56.414
keep somethin' to light a fire on you,

00:09:56.414 --> 00:10:00.103
and a lighter in my
opinion is the best way.

00:10:00.103 --> 00:10:01.732
Another thing, just-in-case item,

00:10:01.732 --> 00:10:03.192
bring some flashlights.

00:10:03.192 --> 00:10:04.988
I have this cheap headlamp here.

00:10:04.988 --> 00:10:06.415
This actually cost $10.

00:10:06.415 --> 00:10:08.851
It's rechargeable, I did a test,

00:10:08.851 --> 00:10:10.851
the battery, I kept it
on the highest setting,

00:10:10.851 --> 00:10:14.871
it lasts for about 11 hours
and it still didn't die

00:10:14.871 --> 00:10:16.300
so I just shut it off at that point,

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:19.079
I determined it was very useful.

00:10:19.079 --> 00:10:20.090
$10 on Amazon,

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:22.479
if I can remember where
I got it from or the name

00:10:22.479 --> 00:10:24.608
I'll put it in the
description for you guys.

00:10:24.608 --> 00:10:26.761
So bring some type of light.

00:10:26.761 --> 00:10:30.178
I have another light on my keychain here.

00:10:31.986 --> 00:10:33.967
It's a very small light
but it's very effective,

00:10:33.967 --> 00:10:36.467
it's a high-powered LED light.

00:10:37.854 --> 00:10:40.572
I'd recommend bringin'
multiple lights if you can,

00:10:40.572 --> 00:10:42.007
small flashlights work fine,

00:10:42.007 --> 00:10:45.272
you don't need anything
hi-tech or expensive.

00:10:45.272 --> 00:10:46.723
And I actually keep another headlamp

00:10:46.723 --> 00:10:48.719
in my pocket here just in case,

00:10:48.719 --> 00:10:51.963
it stays with me at all times
in my jacket just in case.

00:10:51.963 --> 00:10:55.630
This is my better
headlamp that I purchased.

00:10:58.067 --> 00:10:59.437
Just in case you do get lost

00:10:59.437 --> 00:11:00.787
and you're out longer than expected

00:11:00.787 --> 00:11:03.397
and it does become night

00:11:03.397 --> 00:11:07.064
you want to have light
to be able to navigate.

00:11:08.367 --> 00:11:10.192
And that's basically it for day hikes.

00:11:10.192 --> 00:11:12.606
Something I usually bring too,

00:11:12.606 --> 00:11:15.344
just this little foam
pad, just for comfort.

00:11:15.344 --> 00:11:16.708
If I stop to sit down for a second,

00:11:16.708 --> 00:11:19.560
put it on my bum, sit
down, it's nice and cozy

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.130
so my butt doesn't go numb.

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:22.666
So that's somethin' simple there,

00:11:22.666 --> 00:11:25.428
and I also use this to
fan the fire sometimes.

00:11:25.428 --> 00:11:27.870
So just some comfortable
items that I bring.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:31.155
And that's the basics for
a one to three mile hike.

00:11:31.155 --> 00:11:34.213
It's not too extensive,
you don't need a lot

00:11:34.213 --> 00:11:35.954
when you're goin' out hiking.

00:11:35.954 --> 00:11:38.104
Just focus on the area that you're in,

00:11:38.104 --> 00:11:39.274
know the area that you're in,

00:11:39.274 --> 00:11:40.837
and know how to get out, just in case.

00:11:40.837 --> 00:11:44.403
For longer hikes I would
bring all this stuff

00:11:44.403 --> 00:11:46.268
that I just explained.

00:11:46.268 --> 00:11:50.122
I would also bring a water
filter, very important,

00:11:50.122 --> 00:11:53.289
and I would also bring this Mylar top.

00:11:54.531 --> 00:11:57.271
It's great for protection
from the elements,

00:11:57.271 --> 00:11:59.188
if I had to make an emergency shelter

00:11:59.188 --> 00:12:00.891
I could put this up in a lean-to fashion,

00:12:00.891 --> 00:12:02.426
have a fire in front of me,

00:12:02.426 --> 00:12:04.446
and it should keep me
warm the entire night.

00:12:04.446 --> 00:12:06.295
I've actually done it before.

00:12:06.295 --> 00:12:08.159
I highly recommend these Mylar tops.

00:12:08.159 --> 00:12:10.308
I believe it was like $15 on Amazon.

00:12:10.308 --> 00:12:13.789
Lightweight, very very versatile item.

00:12:13.789 --> 00:12:15.433
And they come in a couple
of different colors,

00:12:15.433 --> 00:12:17.835
but this is my favorite
piece of kit that I have

00:12:17.835 --> 00:12:19.400
and I highly recommend it.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:22.632
I bought one, I liked it
so much I bought two more.

00:12:22.632 --> 00:12:23.679
So that says a lot.

00:12:23.679 --> 00:12:24.879
If you're just getting into hiking

00:12:24.879 --> 00:12:27.310
I recommend bring more than you need.

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:29.034
If you can bear the weight on your back,

00:12:29.034 --> 00:12:30.330
bring more than you need

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:31.368
and then over time

00:12:31.368 --> 00:12:33.395
you'll get used to the
gear that you don't need,

00:12:33.395 --> 00:12:36.609
and little by little you'll
have less and less and less.

00:12:36.609 --> 00:12:38.442
That's basically how I

00:12:40.799 --> 00:12:42.316
decided on what gear I should bring.

00:12:42.316 --> 00:12:45.546
I went and my pack was a
little heavy in the beginning,

00:12:45.546 --> 00:12:46.855
but over time I was like,

00:12:46.855 --> 00:12:48.894
"I've never even used this
on the last three hikes

00:12:48.894 --> 00:12:50.111
"so I'm going to take it out of my bag."

00:12:50.111 --> 00:12:53.839
And then you just go on from there.

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:55.106
So that's it.

00:12:55.106 --> 00:12:57.626
Get a nice comfortable backpack.

00:12:57.626 --> 00:12:58.606
Over time obviously

00:12:58.606 --> 00:13:00.389
you don't want to spend
$100 on a backpack.

00:13:00.389 --> 00:13:02.226
If you're goin' out
hikin' for the first time,

00:13:02.226 --> 00:13:04.314
get somethin' cheap and just learn

00:13:04.314 --> 00:13:08.972
and grow and get out there
and experience the woods.

00:13:08.972 --> 00:13:10.592
And tell me about your adventure.

00:13:10.592 --> 00:13:12.950
- Respect the backcountry.

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:13.907
What I mean by this

00:13:13.907 --> 00:13:16.627
is that when I am in the
backcountry traveling alone

00:13:16.627 --> 00:13:20.832
whether I'm scaling a steep
slope to get up a peak

00:13:20.832 --> 00:13:22.731
or I'm crossing a stream,

00:13:22.731 --> 00:13:25.724
I keep a slogan in the back
of my mind, and that is:

00:13:25.724 --> 00:13:27.976
Remember where you are.

00:13:27.976 --> 00:13:30.737
And what that reminds me

00:13:30.737 --> 00:13:34.701
is that if I were to get
hurt right at that moment,

00:13:34.701 --> 00:13:39.223
I am days away, potentially,
from anyone coming to help me.

00:13:39.223 --> 00:13:42.831
So it helps me to be vigilant
when I'm hiking down the trail

00:13:42.831 --> 00:13:46.467
or stepping from boulder to boulder.

00:13:46.467 --> 00:13:49.230
Most times when people have
accidents in the backcountry

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:51.043
is due to laziness.

00:13:51.043 --> 00:13:52.846
They're just not paying attention.

00:13:52.846 --> 00:13:53.679
I mean, I get it.

00:13:53.679 --> 00:13:55.499
You get fatigued, it's late in the day,

00:13:55.499 --> 00:13:57.608
and you're just not paying attention

00:13:57.608 --> 00:14:00.153
when you're puttin' your
full weight down on a rock,

00:14:00.153 --> 00:14:02.067
you just assume that rock is stable,

00:14:02.067 --> 00:14:04.002
and you can't, when
you're traveling alone.

00:14:04.002 --> 00:14:05.990
You get an injury by yourself

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:08.153
and there's nobody there to help you.

00:14:08.153 --> 00:14:10.369
So remembering where you are

00:14:10.369 --> 00:14:12.431
just helps you to be vigilant at all times

00:14:12.431 --> 00:14:15.098
so that you minimize your risks.

00:14:16.084 --> 00:14:18.753
- And then last on the
list is emergency shelter.

00:14:18.753 --> 00:14:20.041
We call 'em a bivy.

00:14:20.041 --> 00:14:22.294
And here's a two-person
bivy in a small container,

00:14:22.294 --> 00:14:23.853
and here's a one-person bivy.

00:14:23.853 --> 00:14:25.285
You can see how small these are,

00:14:25.285 --> 00:14:26.299
you just throw it in your pack,

00:14:26.299 --> 00:14:28.199
and if you never need it
you never know it's there.

00:14:28.199 --> 00:14:30.817
But when you do need it,
it'll save your life.

00:14:30.817 --> 00:14:32.260
So definitely recommend that.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:33.465
One of the things I like to carry

00:14:33.465 --> 00:14:35.184
on some big trips in the north Cascades

00:14:35.184 --> 00:14:36.744
is my SPOT 2 messenger.

00:14:36.744 --> 00:14:38.426
I can check in with my wife and kids,

00:14:38.426 --> 00:14:39.882
let 'em know that I'm okay,

00:14:39.882 --> 00:14:41.725
and if I do need help
I can hit the button.

00:14:41.725 --> 00:14:44.666
It works with satellites,
and it works very well.

00:14:44.666 --> 00:14:47.005
It's been out for a long time.

00:14:47.005 --> 00:14:49.984
I also recommend that you
maybe bring a plastic bag.

00:14:49.984 --> 00:14:52.905
This is great for shelter as
well as keepin' everything dry

00:14:52.905 --> 00:14:54.283
if you're in a torrential downpour.

00:14:54.283 --> 00:14:57.598
You get wet, you get
cold, you're in trouble.

00:14:57.598 --> 00:14:58.980
Things to think about is weight.

00:14:58.980 --> 00:14:59.862
If you go on a trip,

00:14:59.862 --> 00:15:01.398
you obviously got to keep your weight

00:15:01.398 --> 00:15:03.103
in proportion to what you can carry.

00:15:03.103 --> 00:15:04.854
So scale down or scale up things

00:15:04.854 --> 00:15:06.199
depending on where you're going.

00:15:06.199 --> 00:15:07.470
Use common sense.

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:09.750
Are you takin' a long
trip, are there hazards,

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:11.709
is there going to be weather problems?

00:15:11.709 --> 00:15:13.292
Be able to deal with everything.

00:15:13.292 --> 00:15:15.002
Nobody wants to call search and rescue

00:15:15.002 --> 00:15:17.280
and nobody wants to find themselves

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.057
spending an unwanted
night in the wilderness.

00:15:20.057 --> 00:15:23.140
(light upbeat music)

00:15:24.278 --> 00:15:27.767
- What are the main categories
or classes of hiking boots?

00:15:27.767 --> 00:15:30.664
- There's four main styles and
categories of hiking boots.

00:15:30.664 --> 00:15:33.937
The first would be the low-cut,
ultra-light hiking boots,

00:15:33.937 --> 00:15:36.788
and then it would be just a
general, all-purpose hiker.

00:15:36.788 --> 00:15:38.396
And then you're going to go
with a little bit heavier

00:15:38.396 --> 00:15:39.989
in a backpacking hiking boot,

00:15:39.989 --> 00:15:41.703
and then the heaviest and most durable

00:15:41.703 --> 00:15:44.221
would be the mountaineering
and winter hiking boots.

00:15:44.221 --> 00:15:47.562
The ultra-light hiking
boots are designed for

00:15:47.562 --> 00:15:49.049
the backpacker who wants to cut down

00:15:49.049 --> 00:15:51.440
on their weight on their trips.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:53.523
Most of them are not waterproof,

00:15:53.523 --> 00:15:55.435
they're mostly lightweight, breathable,

00:15:55.435 --> 00:15:58.353
but they provide for easier
comfort through the day,

00:15:58.353 --> 00:16:00.444
they're going to keep your feet dry,

00:16:00.444 --> 00:16:01.482
even if you go through water

00:16:01.482 --> 00:16:02.703
they're going to breathe a lot faster

00:16:02.703 --> 00:16:04.431
so you are going to dry out quicker.

00:16:04.431 --> 00:16:06.181
Most of 'em do have a
pretty aggressive tread

00:16:06.181 --> 00:16:07.560
to kind of keep going through

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:09.647
most of the rugged terrain in Idaho.

00:16:09.647 --> 00:16:10.600
A lot of customers

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:12.148
or a lot of people who
are going out hiking

00:16:12.148 --> 00:16:15.485
prefer to not have ankle support.

00:16:15.485 --> 00:16:17.390
A lot of people do.

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:19.819
People with ankle injuries especially

00:16:19.819 --> 00:16:21.999
don't like to have the hightop boots

00:16:21.999 --> 00:16:25.675
because it rubs on scars or scar tissue.

00:16:25.675 --> 00:16:28.055
And so we get a lot of people
asking for a lower cut,

00:16:28.055 --> 00:16:30.796
still aggressive and still rugged shoe.

00:16:30.796 --> 00:16:33.962
- Now, I've heard that
one pound on your feet

00:16:33.962 --> 00:16:36.807
actually equals five pounds on your back.

00:16:36.807 --> 00:16:37.704
Is that true?

00:16:37.704 --> 00:16:38.717
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:16:38.717 --> 00:16:40.792
The feet are moving all day,

00:16:40.792 --> 00:16:43.611
and if you think about
lugging one of these around

00:16:43.611 --> 00:16:47.088
as opposed to one of
these, it's way different.

00:16:47.088 --> 00:16:48.664
This is three pounds a foot,

00:16:48.664 --> 00:16:51.115
this is not even 16 ounces,

00:16:51.115 --> 00:16:52.793
it'd be like 12 ounces a foot.

00:16:52.793 --> 00:16:54.698
So you're cutting a lot of weight,

00:16:54.698 --> 00:16:56.179
and they're moving all day.

00:16:56.179 --> 00:16:57.444
And so at the end of the day

00:16:57.444 --> 00:16:59.914
you're going to have a lot
more energy wearing these out

00:16:59.914 --> 00:17:02.074
than you are when you
wear these heavier-duty

00:17:02.074 --> 00:17:03.151
mountaineering boots.

00:17:03.151 --> 00:17:06.719
- Seeing I'm more of a
heavy-weight backpacker

00:17:06.719 --> 00:17:08.632
I go on a lot rougher terrain,

00:17:08.632 --> 00:17:11.181
I'm carrying weights of 30, 40,

00:17:11.181 --> 00:17:13.391
maybe even 50 pounds or more,

00:17:13.391 --> 00:17:16.318
what type of boot am I going to
step up to in that situation?

00:17:16.318 --> 00:17:17.701
- When you're in that situation

00:17:17.701 --> 00:17:20.531
you're going to step up to an
all-around backpacking boot.

00:17:20.531 --> 00:17:22.061
Most of them are an all-leather

00:17:22.061 --> 00:17:24.148
so you're going to get
a lot of waterproofing,

00:17:24.148 --> 00:17:25.954
you can use a Nikwax
or something like that

00:17:25.954 --> 00:17:28.180
to also waterproof them as well.

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:30.022
And most of them have Vibram soles,

00:17:30.022 --> 00:17:32.366
and so they're going to provide
a little bit more traction,

00:17:32.366 --> 00:17:33.872
and a little more stability.

00:17:33.872 --> 00:17:35.574
And it's a lot wider of a base

00:17:35.574 --> 00:17:37.481
than a traditional hiking shoe,

00:17:37.481 --> 00:17:39.998
and so it's going to provide
that stability left and right.

00:17:39.998 --> 00:17:42.282
One of the main things to keep in mind

00:17:42.282 --> 00:17:43.406
which a lot of people don't know

00:17:43.406 --> 00:17:46.304
is trying on boots
later on during the day.

00:17:46.304 --> 00:17:49.634
Your feet tend to swell
as you're standing around

00:17:49.634 --> 00:17:50.527
and walking around,

00:17:50.527 --> 00:17:52.610
so knowing that your feet

00:17:53.475 --> 00:17:54.868
are going to be a little bit different,

00:17:54.868 --> 00:17:57.443
even a half size different
later in the day,

00:17:57.443 --> 00:18:00.417
is going to help when you are
tired at the end of a hike.

00:18:00.417 --> 00:18:01.944
So trying on shoes later in the day

00:18:01.944 --> 00:18:04.557
definitely helps cuz
it's more of a true fit.

00:18:04.557 --> 00:18:06.999
- Is there anything else I can do in-store

00:18:06.999 --> 00:18:09.633
to make sure that the
boot is going to work?

00:18:09.633 --> 00:18:14.270
I know some stores offer
incline ramps or other devices.

00:18:14.270 --> 00:18:16.111
Can you maybe speak to that just quickly?

00:18:16.111 --> 00:18:16.944
- Absolutely.

00:18:16.944 --> 00:18:19.245
An incline ramp is crucial
when trying out a boot.

00:18:19.245 --> 00:18:20.791
You definitely want to lace your boot up

00:18:20.791 --> 00:18:21.730
like you're going to wear it,

00:18:21.730 --> 00:18:25.185
and then start at the top and
stutter step your way down

00:18:25.185 --> 00:18:27.105
and make sure that your toes
aren't hitting the front.

00:18:27.105 --> 00:18:28.305
Cuz if they're hitting the front

00:18:28.305 --> 00:18:29.998
four or five steps down an incline ramp,

00:18:29.998 --> 00:18:32.584
they're going to hit on the
way down from a mountain.

00:18:32.584 --> 00:18:34.132
And then you turn around and walk back up

00:18:34.132 --> 00:18:36.666
and make sure that your heel
isn't slipping in the back,

00:18:36.666 --> 00:18:38.564
cuz that would cause a
blister pretty quickly.

00:18:38.564 --> 00:18:41.696
- So once I've actually tested the shoes,

00:18:41.696 --> 00:18:43.496
picked 'em out, purchased them,

00:18:43.496 --> 00:18:46.616
what do I need to do prior to
hitting the trail with them?

00:18:46.616 --> 00:18:49.093
Do I just break 'em out of
the box and go for a hike?

00:18:49.093 --> 00:18:50.325
What do I do?

00:18:50.325 --> 00:18:53.203
- Prior to taking them
out on your first trip

00:18:53.203 --> 00:18:54.087
or your first adventure

00:18:54.087 --> 00:18:56.213
would be just wearing
them around the house

00:18:56.213 --> 00:18:57.568
for a short amount of time.

00:18:57.568 --> 00:18:59.191
A lot of these, the stiffer ones,

00:18:59.191 --> 00:19:01.487
are going to take a long time to break in.

00:19:01.487 --> 00:19:03.756
Some people have had 'em
on for two, three years

00:19:03.756 --> 00:19:05.303
and still haven't broken 'em in.

00:19:05.303 --> 00:19:08.527
So definitely taking them on
short walks around the block.

00:19:08.527 --> 00:19:10.465
If you've got a dog and
you're walking a dog,

00:19:10.465 --> 00:19:13.016
or even taking them on little mini hikes,

00:19:13.016 --> 00:19:14.332
that way you're breaking them in

00:19:14.332 --> 00:19:17.172
but you're still not committed to them

00:19:17.172 --> 00:19:19.194
when you're out there and
you don't have anything else

00:19:19.194 --> 00:19:20.245
to change into.

00:19:20.245 --> 00:19:22.232
- You only need a handful of garments

00:19:22.232 --> 00:19:24.635
to stay warm, dry, and comfortable

00:19:24.635 --> 00:19:26.772
in rain, wind, or snow

00:19:26.772 --> 00:19:29.939
so that you can enjoy views like this.

00:19:31.634 --> 00:19:33.421
Hi, I'm Jonathon Simons

00:19:33.421 --> 00:19:36.280
and I'm a wildlife tracker
and bushcraft instructor.

00:19:36.280 --> 00:19:39.638
And in my spare time, a keen
birdwathcher and hill walker.

00:19:39.638 --> 00:19:42.184
So I spend a lot of my time outdoors.

00:19:42.184 --> 00:19:43.948
Every time I do go out,

00:19:43.948 --> 00:19:46.810
I use the same versatile clothing system

00:19:46.810 --> 00:19:48.531
known as the layering system

00:19:48.531 --> 00:19:51.773
to keep me warm, dry, and comfortable.

00:19:51.773 --> 00:19:54.775
And today, I'm going to
show you how it works.

00:19:54.775 --> 00:19:56.806
I'm working with my friends at Go Outdoors

00:19:56.806 --> 00:19:59.087
who have all the know-how on outdoor kit

00:19:59.087 --> 00:20:00.448
at their fingertips.

00:20:00.448 --> 00:20:01.901
The light and packable clothing

00:20:01.901 --> 00:20:03.398
that forms the layering system

00:20:03.398 --> 00:20:05.442
is designed to be kept in your rucksack

00:20:05.442 --> 00:20:08.305
so that it can be pulled
out at a moment's notice.

00:20:08.305 --> 00:20:09.839
To see how it works,

00:20:09.839 --> 00:20:13.017
we need to rewind to the start of the day.

00:20:13.017 --> 00:20:16.237
(tape rewinding)

00:20:16.237 --> 00:20:19.980
The first part of the layering
system is the base layer.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:22.839
It's designed to trap a
thin layer of warm air

00:20:22.839 --> 00:20:23.734
against my body

00:20:23.734 --> 00:20:27.999
and pools, or wicks, sweat
vapor away from the skin.

00:20:27.999 --> 00:20:31.594
It also keeps me very warm and dry.

00:20:31.594 --> 00:20:34.507
I'm wearing a base layer
made of merino wool.

00:20:34.507 --> 00:20:37.798
Its fibers are so fine it
doesn't itch or scratch.

00:20:37.798 --> 00:20:41.048
Merino also helps neutralize body odor.

00:20:41.909 --> 00:20:45.801
And it's an environmentally-friendly,
renewable fabric.

00:20:45.801 --> 00:20:48.757
There's one base layer fabric
that you need to avoid,

00:20:48.757 --> 00:20:50.384
and that's cotton.

00:20:50.384 --> 00:20:52.762
Cotton soaks up sweat like a sponge,

00:20:52.762 --> 00:20:54.050
and this is bad news

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:55.821
because when cotton gets wet,

00:20:55.821 --> 00:20:58.374
it draws heat away from your body.

00:20:58.374 --> 00:21:00.975
And this can lead to hypothermia.

00:21:00.975 --> 00:21:04.382
Hypothermia is a potentially
lethal condition,

00:21:04.382 --> 00:21:07.854
and can be contracted in
surprisingly mild weather.

00:21:07.854 --> 00:21:09.588
So unless you're in a hot climate,

00:21:09.588 --> 00:21:12.098
avoid wearing cotton clothing.

00:21:12.098 --> 00:21:16.265
And of course, avoid wearing
cotton underwear and socks too.

00:21:17.391 --> 00:21:18.400
Well, it's getting cooler

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:21.027
so it's time to throw on the
next part of my layering system

00:21:21.027 --> 00:21:22.944
which is the mid layer.

00:21:24.329 --> 00:21:26.666
Now, the mid layer is
designed to trap body heat

00:21:26.666 --> 00:21:28.279
and to allow sweat vapor

00:21:28.279 --> 00:21:30.801
to carry on moving through
the layering system.

00:21:30.801 --> 00:21:33.492
The most popular mid
layer fabric is fleece,

00:21:33.492 --> 00:21:36.675
as it's affordable,
durable, and quick-drying.

00:21:36.675 --> 00:21:39.798
The thicker the fleece,
the more heat it traps.

00:21:39.798 --> 00:21:42.831
So you want a mid-weight
fleece for colder weather,

00:21:42.831 --> 00:21:45.288
and a thin, or micro-fleece like this one

00:21:45.288 --> 00:21:47.205
for milder conditions.

00:21:47.205 --> 00:21:50.304
As you gain altitude
the temperature drops,

00:21:50.304 --> 00:21:53.634
so remember, on the summit
it could be quite cold,

00:21:53.634 --> 00:21:57.613
even though it was really
warm down in the car park.

00:21:57.613 --> 00:22:00.899
But ambient temperature's
only part of the story.

00:22:00.899 --> 00:22:03.109
Wind and rain can have a big impact

00:22:03.109 --> 00:22:07.026
on just how cold and
uncomfortable it can feel.

00:22:08.759 --> 00:22:09.920
Which is where the next part

00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:12.208
of the layering system comes in.

00:22:12.208 --> 00:22:14.360
This outer layer, known as the shell,

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.520
blocks wind and rain.

00:22:16.520 --> 00:22:19.764
It also allows my sweat vapor
to leave the layering system.

00:22:19.764 --> 00:22:22.715
I'm wearing a waterproof Paramo shell,

00:22:22.715 --> 00:22:25.072
and I'm feeling very comfortable indeed.

00:22:25.072 --> 00:22:26.513
Lovely.

00:22:26.513 --> 00:22:28.490
- There are three types of shell:

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:31.377
Paramo, membrane, and coated nylon.

00:22:31.377 --> 00:22:34.086
They all do a great job in
keeping out the elements,

00:22:34.086 --> 00:22:36.824
but they vary in how quickly
they allow sweat vapor

00:22:36.824 --> 00:22:40.166
to move from the inside to
the outside of the garment.

00:22:40.166 --> 00:22:42.439
Paramo's Nikwax Analogy fabric,

00:22:42.439 --> 00:22:44.025
which is what John is wearing,

00:22:44.025 --> 00:22:44.999
and membranes,

00:22:44.999 --> 00:22:47.979
are the two types of waterproof
and breathable material

00:22:47.979 --> 00:22:50.415
that are best suited for active use.

00:22:50.415 --> 00:22:52.455
Paramo is very breathable,

00:22:52.455 --> 00:22:54.216
and great at keeping you comfortable

00:22:54.216 --> 00:22:56.013
in many weather conditions.

00:22:56.013 --> 00:22:57.504
But the material is heavier

00:22:57.504 --> 00:23:00.392
than some membranes and coated nylon.

00:23:00.392 --> 00:23:03.818
Membranes such as Gore-Tex,
eVent, and Dew Point

00:23:03.818 --> 00:23:06.151
are lighter and more packable than Paramo,

00:23:06.151 --> 00:23:08.627
but they don't breathe
quite as effectively.

00:23:08.627 --> 00:23:11.239
The third type of outer
layer is a coated nylon.

00:23:11.239 --> 00:23:13.628
These shells are designed
for passive activity

00:23:13.628 --> 00:23:15.018
such as walking the dog,

00:23:15.018 --> 00:23:16.663
short rambles with a day sack,

00:23:16.663 --> 00:23:18.572
or hiking to the pub.

00:23:18.572 --> 00:23:21.755
Coated nylons are less
expensive and less breathable

00:23:21.755 --> 00:23:23.430
than Paramo or membranes,

00:23:23.430 --> 00:23:26.466
and will feel sweaty
during arduous activities.

00:23:26.466 --> 00:23:30.171
- You can use the layering
system on your legs too.

00:23:30.171 --> 00:23:33.136
You can layer a pair of
merino or synthetic leggings

00:23:33.136 --> 00:23:36.817
underneath your wind-resistant
summer trekking trousers,

00:23:36.817 --> 00:23:38.423
and you can pull on a pair

00:23:38.423 --> 00:23:41.435
of breathable and waterproof overtrousers

00:23:41.435 --> 00:23:43.613
should the weather come in.

00:23:43.613 --> 00:23:45.822
An alternative to the
legwear layering system

00:23:45.822 --> 00:23:49.228
is a pair of soft-shell
trousers like these.

00:23:49.228 --> 00:23:51.509
Soft-shell is a popular legwear material,

00:23:51.509 --> 00:23:53.124
as it's time-consuming

00:23:53.124 --> 00:23:55.932
to adjust multiple layers on the move.

00:23:55.932 --> 00:23:58.733
- Hi there guys, I'm out
here enjoying nature again,

00:23:58.733 --> 00:24:02.281
just next to a nice little
village on the Alps.

00:24:02.281 --> 00:24:04.821
And because I'm always out and about

00:24:04.821 --> 00:24:06.318
doing all sorts of adventures,

00:24:06.318 --> 00:24:09.651
a few people are asking me about hiking.

00:24:10.736 --> 00:24:14.352
Like "What sort of gear do
you take when you go hiking,

00:24:14.352 --> 00:24:16.733
"overnight camping, and so on?

00:24:16.733 --> 00:24:19.560
"What do you need, what do you
actually take when you go?"

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:22.478
So here I've selected 15 items

00:24:22.478 --> 00:24:24.573
that I consider as the basic kit,

00:24:24.573 --> 00:24:26.055
things that you should be taking

00:24:26.055 --> 00:24:28.868
if you go on that sort of activity.

00:24:28.868 --> 00:24:33.364
So let's get started with
number one, is your tent.

00:24:33.364 --> 00:24:36.773
This might be for one
to three or more people,

00:24:36.773 --> 00:24:38.890
maybe three seasons, four season,

00:24:38.890 --> 00:24:40.365
it all depends on what sort of conditions

00:24:40.365 --> 00:24:42.247
you're going to be facing.

00:24:42.247 --> 00:24:44.644
Number two is some sort of isolating mat.

00:24:44.644 --> 00:24:45.951
The main function of this

00:24:45.951 --> 00:24:48.673
is not only to give you some
comfort against the hard ground

00:24:48.673 --> 00:24:50.149
underneath your sleeping bag,

00:24:50.149 --> 00:24:53.950
but also to protect you
from cold and humidity

00:24:53.950 --> 00:24:55.790
coming from the ground.

00:24:55.790 --> 00:24:59.616
Talking about cold, number
three is your sleeping bag.

00:24:59.616 --> 00:25:01.998
Make sure that the temperature
rating of your sleeping bag

00:25:01.998 --> 00:25:05.135
is adequate to the temperatures
that you're going to be facing.

00:25:05.135 --> 00:25:06.534
Consider that at night time

00:25:06.534 --> 00:25:10.053
the temperature can drop quite a lot.

00:25:10.053 --> 00:25:12.324
Number four is your kitchen.

00:25:12.324 --> 00:25:14.729
It's a kitchen kit, really.

00:25:14.729 --> 00:25:16.584
So here's your stove.

00:25:16.584 --> 00:25:17.894
Several models out there:

00:25:17.894 --> 00:25:21.111
Gas, alcohol, multi-fuel, and so on.

00:25:21.111 --> 00:25:23.611
Your pots and eating utensils.

00:25:25.286 --> 00:25:28.105
Number five is one of my favorites

00:25:28.105 --> 00:25:29.074
is your food package,

00:25:29.074 --> 00:25:31.626
breakfast, lunch, dinner,
trail snacks, and so on.

00:25:31.626 --> 00:25:35.457
I'm going to be talking about
all this gear on another video,

00:25:35.457 --> 00:25:38.542
giving more details and being
more specific, but yeah.

00:25:38.542 --> 00:25:41.003
So that was number five.

00:25:41.003 --> 00:25:43.026
Number six is water bottles.

00:25:43.026 --> 00:25:45.604
Not only to keep you
hydrated during the walk

00:25:45.604 --> 00:25:46.581
and during the camping,

00:25:46.581 --> 00:25:50.420
but also to measure water for cooking,

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:52.361
and a lot of them can be used

00:25:52.361 --> 00:25:55.029
with water purification systems.

00:25:55.029 --> 00:25:57.624
Which is our next item, number seven.

00:25:57.624 --> 00:25:59.511
This could be a filter,

00:25:59.511 --> 00:26:02.275
you can use water purification tablets,

00:26:02.275 --> 00:26:04.153
maybe even boil water,

00:26:04.153 --> 00:26:05.897
but it's quite important to treat water.

00:26:05.897 --> 00:26:07.346
You don't want to get contaminated,

00:26:07.346 --> 00:26:11.003
especially when you're
camping on remote areas.

00:26:11.003 --> 00:26:13.181
Number eight is a torch.

00:26:13.181 --> 00:26:15.362
I personally like headlamps.

00:26:15.362 --> 00:26:18.791
Hands-free, walking at night,
cooking after dark, and so on.

00:26:18.791 --> 00:26:22.064
Very handy, make sure
you take spare batteries.

00:26:22.064 --> 00:26:24.667
Number nine is a first aid kit

00:26:24.667 --> 00:26:26.502
with a multi-tool or a knife.

00:26:26.502 --> 00:26:29.554
I make sure that this is
always in a waterproof bag,

00:26:29.554 --> 00:26:31.881
I do the same with the rest of my gear,

00:26:31.881 --> 00:26:34.638
but particularly with this one here.

00:26:34.638 --> 00:26:37.860
Number 10 is your personal hygiene kit.

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:41.561
This is where your
toothpaste, toothbrush is.

00:26:41.561 --> 00:26:44.636
You might want to take a
small microfiber towel,

00:26:44.636 --> 00:26:48.803
and your toilet paper also
kept in a small dry bag.

00:26:49.683 --> 00:26:52.707
Number 11 is your clothing.

00:26:52.707 --> 00:26:56.124
Just a package of fresh, dry, warm layers

00:26:57.115 --> 00:26:59.377
so if the temperature drops

00:26:59.377 --> 00:27:02.497
you want to make sure
you're warm and protected,

00:27:02.497 --> 00:27:03.734
and that's it.

00:27:03.734 --> 00:27:07.604
So talking about protection,
we got to number 12,

00:27:07.604 --> 00:27:10.390
which is a rain jacket and rain pants.

00:27:10.390 --> 00:27:13.034
When we're outdoors, we're always exposed.

00:27:13.034 --> 00:27:15.284
Rain, wind, snow and so on.

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:20.323
You want to make sure you're dry and warm.

00:27:20.323 --> 00:27:24.490
They also give you a lot of
protection against the wind.

00:27:27.283 --> 00:27:31.450
The next item is a map covering
the entire area and compass,

00:27:32.475 --> 00:27:36.642
and ideally some basic
orienteering skills as well.

00:27:39.774 --> 00:27:41.833
Alright, number 14 is a small pouch

00:27:41.833 --> 00:27:45.678
with your wallet, phone,
and keys, and so on.

00:27:45.678 --> 00:27:48.831
Here you can also keep
copies of reservations

00:27:48.831 --> 00:27:50.728
like an entry for a national park

00:27:50.728 --> 00:27:54.756
or some sort of ticket for
transportation and so on.

00:27:54.756 --> 00:27:56.923
And lastly, our last item,

00:27:59.639 --> 00:28:01.472
number 15, a backpack.

00:28:03.403 --> 00:28:06.388
Obviously something
appropriate for hiking.

00:28:06.388 --> 00:28:08.928
It must be comfortable and
with capacity big enough

00:28:08.928 --> 00:28:11.477
for all your gear, and
maybe a little bit more

00:28:11.477 --> 00:28:14.861
so that you don't have to pack
everything extremely precise,

00:28:14.861 --> 00:28:18.023
you've got a little bit of
margin to play around with.

00:28:18.023 --> 00:28:20.807
Right, so these are pretty
much the 15 basic items

00:28:20.807 --> 00:28:23.225
that I consider as the
basic kit for hiking,

00:28:23.225 --> 00:28:24.956
staying overnight.

00:28:24.956 --> 00:28:27.694
- Thank you guys so much for
watching, I hope you enjoyed.

00:28:27.694 --> 00:28:29.626
I'd love to know what did
you think of this video,

00:28:29.626 --> 00:28:31.504
what was the message that you took home

00:28:31.504 --> 00:28:34.818
that you're going to immediately
apply somehow in your life

00:28:34.818 --> 00:28:36.213
or maybe in your business.

00:28:36.213 --> 00:28:37.708
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:28:37.708 --> 00:28:39.035
I'm really curious to find out.

00:28:39.035 --> 00:28:40.974
I also want to give real
quick credit to Edward.

00:28:40.974 --> 00:28:41.990
Edward, thank you so much

00:28:41.990 --> 00:28:44.251
for picking a copy of
my book Your One Word.

00:28:44.251 --> 00:28:45.630
I hope you're enjoying the read, man,

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:47.695
and I'm so appreciative for your support.

00:28:47.695 --> 00:28:49.019
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:28:49.019 --> 00:28:50.123
I believe in you,

00:28:50.123 --> 00:28:52.224
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:28:52.224 --> 00:28:54.232
and whatever your one word is,

00:28:54.232 --> 00:28:56.795
much love, have an amazing day,

00:28:56.795 --> 00:28:58.621
and I'll see you soon.

00:28:58.621 --> 00:29:00.954
